  "coach.fallback": "I am a rule-based coach, not a general AI, so I may have missed how you phrased that. Try one of the buttons below, or ask about a Go term, the rules or the position.",
  "commentary.idea.capture": "Idea: a captured stone is gone for good. Before each move, ask: can I capture something safely?",
  "commentary.idea.rescue": "Idea: check for atari before every move. A group with one liberty can vanish in a single move.",
  "commentary.idea.atari": "Idea: atari is a threat, not a capture. It works best when the group cannot escape, or when the reply it forces helps you.",
  "commentary.idea.connect": "Idea: connected stones share their liberties. Stones left apart can be attacked one at a time.",
  "commentary.idea.cut": "Idea: cutting turns one strong group into two weak ones, and weak groups have to spend moves defending.",
  "commentary.idea.territory": "Idea: your score is your stones plus the empty points you surround, so claiming big empty areas early is efficient.",
  "commentary.idea.approach": "Idea: a stone placed near the opponent’s stone limits how much space that stone can claim.",
  "commentary.idea.build": "Idea: stones that support each other make a position strong, and a strong position makes territory easier to hold.",
  "commentary.idea.boundary": "Idea: where two sides meet, each move decides how far each side’s area reaches. Moves on the boundary are often the biggest on the board.",
  "commentary.idea.line": "Idea: the third line is the \"line of territory\" (safe and good for points) and the fourth is the \"line of influence\" (more power towards the centre).",
  "commentary.checklist": "Five questions to ask before every move:\n1. Is any group in atari, mine or my opponent’s?\n2. Can I capture something, or must I rescue something?\n3. Are my groups connected, or can the opponent cut between them?\n4. Where is the biggest empty area, and can I claim it?\n5. Will my new stone have enough liberties to be safe?\nAnswer them in that order: danger first, then opportunity, then territory.",
